            <ArticleTitle>Functional and molecular imaging: applications for diagnosis and staging of localised prostate cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>451-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clon.2013.05.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0936-6555(13)00208-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Prostate cancer is currently the most common solid organ cancer type among men in the Western world. Currently, all decision-making algorithms and nomograms rely on demographics, clinicopathological data and symptoms. Such an approach can easily miss significant cancers while detecting many insignificant cancers. In this review, novel functional and molecular imaging techniques used in the diagnosis and staging of localised prostate cancer and their effect on treatment decisions are discussed.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
